WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.400 --> 00:00:07.360
In the first film, a membrane ligand signaled inward through a tail or partner.

2
00:00:08.800 --> 00:00:12.780
Now suppose you delete that ligand's gene and an organ fails to form.

3
00:00:14.120 --> 00:00:16.400
How can you tell which direction caused the defect?

4
00:00:17.760 --> 00:00:19.780
One knockout removes too much at once.

5
00:00:20.783 --> 00:00:24.343
Watch what vanishes when you delete the ligand gene completely.

6
00:00:25.863 --> 00:00:28.703
First, the receptor cell loses its forward signal.

7
00:00:29.943 --> 00:00:33.103
Second, the ligand cell loses its reverse signal.

8
00:00:34.303 --> 00:00:37.683
Third, both cells lose the physical grip holding them together.

9
00:00:39.083 --> 00:00:42.443
Deleting the receptor gene creates the exact same tangle.

10
00:00:43.467 --> 00:00:48.447
To separate bidirectional signals, disable one inner route while keeping binding.

11
00:00:49.887 --> 00:00:54.007
Leave the outer binding tip on the cell surface so the two cells still grip.

12
00:00:55.267 --> 00:01:00.227
Then cut or swap only one cell's intracellular signaling part, keeping the other

13
00:01:00.227 --> 00:01:01.227
side intact.

14
00:01:02.250 --> 00:01:04.990
Watch this rule solve a brain wiring puzzle.

15
00:01:06.670 --> 00:01:12.210
Growing axons carrying B-type ephrins cross floor cells carrying the receptor EphB2.

16
00:01:13.530 --> 00:01:17.430
In mice lacking EphB2 completely, those axons veer off course.

17
00:01:18.750 --> 00:01:21.770
A second mouse keeps EphB2's outer tip on the surface.

18
00:01:22.883 --> 00:01:28.063
In that second mouse, an inert bacterial enzyme replaces the receptor's inner

19
00:01:28.063 --> 00:01:32.903
kinase. That change removes the receptor's own kinase signal.

20
00:01:34.183 --> 00:01:36.343
Yet the axons still stay right on course.

21
00:01:37.743 --> 00:01:40.803
Pause and think: which cell was receiving the guidance signal?

22
00:01:46.717 --> 00:01:51.977
The axons carrying the ephrin received the signal, because the only working part

23
00:01:51.977 --> 00:01:54.177
left was the receptor's outer tip.

24
00:01:55.417 --> 00:02:00.137
That tip stayed on the floor cells and gripped the axons' ephrins, so the message

25
00:02:00.137 --> 00:02:05.477
ran inward there. Here the protein named receptor acted as the ligand.

26
00:02:06.467 --> 00:02:09.827
Mutant animals test whether one direction is needed.

27
00:02:11.047 --> 00:02:14.827
A second tool tests whether reverse signaling is enough on its own.

28
00:02:16.627 --> 00:02:21.227
Scientists fuse a receptor's outer tip to an antibody handle, making a soluble

29
00:02:21.227 --> 00:02:27.767
receptor body. With no receptor cell present, a response that needs the ligand must

30
00:02:27.767 --> 00:02:28.887
have entered through it.

31
00:02:29.850 --> 00:02:35.290
For example, developing sympathetic axons carry uncut membrane TNF.

32
00:02:36.590 --> 00:02:41.290
Adding soluble TNF receptor 1 makes those axons branch more.

33
00:02:42.510 --> 00:02:45.750
That growth requires membrane TNF on the axon.

34
00:02:46.910 --> 00:02:50.670
If a protease cuts TNF loose, that inward link breaks.

35
00:02:52.170 --> 00:02:55.090
Released TNF can still float off and signal forward.

36
00:02:56.283 --> 00:03:01.523
Full knockouts erase forward signaling, reverse signaling, and cell contact all at

37
00:03:01.523 --> 00:03:06.843
once. Keep outer binding intact and disable one inner route at a time.

38
00:03:08.103 --> 00:03:10.923
Only then can you prove which cell received the message.
